; Http - an HTTP/1.1 request parser, a response writer, a router and a
; static-file server, over `Sys`'s sockets and file descriptors.
;
; One connection is one request and one response, and the response
; says so: every reply carries `Connection: close` and the server
; closes the socket. That is the whole of this module's concurrency
; model and it is a decision rather than an omission - see KEEP-ALIVE
; below. A server built on it is the shape tests/net/echo-server.ax
; and examples/web/server.ax have: accept, `netSetBlocking`, mark the
; arena, `httpServeOne`, close, reset (docs/memory-model.md
; MM-ALLOC-22).
;
; READING. `Sys.sysReadFd` performs ONE `read`, and a read on a socket
; answers whatever the peer has flushed: the request line, the
; headers, the terminator and the first bytes of the body routinely
; arrive together, and the rest of the body in several more reads - or
; the request line alone, one byte at a time. So `httpRead` keeps a
; buffer (`Rpc.ax`'s four-word reader, for the same reason its header
; gives), refills until `\r\n\r\n` has arrived, parses the head out of
; the buffer, and then reads exactly `Content-Length` bytes of body
; into a buffer of exactly that size. The reader's buffer starts at
; `httpReadCap` and doubles up to `httpMaxHead`; a head that has not
; ended by then is refused.
;
; MEASURED, so the reader's size is a decision and not a habit. Bytes
; the allocator's bump moved for ONE request, read off
; `__axiom_arena_mark`'s cell between the accept and the close, one
; process over the loopback (2026-08-29). The head is 70 bytes as
; python's http.client sends it, or 764 bytes as Chrome sends it with
; three cookies; the page is examples/web's index, 606 bytes:
;
;     httpRead alone, initial buffer    70-byte head   764-byte head
;         8192                               9,600          12,432
;         4096                               5,504               -
;         2048                               3,456           6,288
;         1024                               2,432           5,312
;          256                               1,664               -
;     rendering the page through Html        4,768
;     httpRespond, a 6-byte text body          656
;     httpServeOne, text route, at 8192     10,016
;     httpServeOne, the page,   at 8192     14,752          17,552
;     httpServeOne, text route, at 2048      3,872
;     httpServeOne, the page,   at 2048      8,608          11,408
;
; At 8 KiB the EMPTY buffer was two thirds of everything a page
; request allocated. `httpReadCap` is 2048: both heads fit in one read
; with no growth, and a head above 2 KiB - many cookies - costs one
; doubling and one 2 KiB copy. The arena reset reclaims all of it
; either way (MM-ALLOC-22), which is exactly why the memory gate
; cannot see this number and this header has to carry it.
;
; THREE REFUSALS THE PARSER CARRIES, all from `Rpc.ax`'s measured
; lesson about a length field a peer writes:
;
;   - `Content-Length` above `httpMaxBody` (8 MiB) is refused as 413,
;     and refused BY `Str.strParseInt`, which tests overflow per digit
;     and answers `None` for `18446744073709551621` - the value that
;     wrapped to 5 and was ACCEPTED by an accumulator with no test;
;   - a request head above `httpMaxHead` (16 KiB) is refused as 431,
;     so a peer cannot make the reader grow without bound;
;   - a header line with no `:` is refused as 400 rather than skipped,
;     as is a bare `\n` line ending, a request line that is not three
;     tokens, and a version that is not HTTP/1.0 or HTTP/1.1 (505).
;
; The error a refusal answers carries the HTTP STATUS as its code, so
; `httpServeOne` writes the refusal to the peer with no table between
; the parser and the wire.
;
; WRITING. `httpRespond` writes the status line, `Content-Type`,
; `Content-Length` (the body's `strLen`, in bytes), `Connection:
; close`, the blank line and the body, through `Sys.sysWriteAllFd` -
; never `sysWriteFd`, because a short write on a socket is normal and
; a writer that took one for success would truncate the reply. No
; `Date` header: it would make every golden non-reproducible, and a
; server with no cache does not need one. `httpRespondRaw` takes an
; address and a length rather than a `String` for one reason:
; `strCStr` stops at the first NUL, so a `.png` served through any
; C-string path is silently corrupt. Binary bodies go out as
; `(strData s)` and `(strLen s)`.
;
; ROUTING. A router holds exact `(method, path)` routes with a
; handler each, static prefixes mapped to directories, and a
; not-found handler. A handler is a function VALUE, `(-> Int HttpReq
; Int)` taking the socket and the request, held in an `HttpHandler`
; cell because a `Vec` holds words and a word is not a type. Write it
; as `(HttpFn (lambda (fd r) (index fd r)))`: a top-level function
; of two parameters is not a value by itself (AX3013), and a lambda
; parameter has no type until it is passed to a signed function, so
; the lambda is a thin wrapper and the handler proper is an ordinary
; signed `fn` - which is also what gives it a real effect row and a
; place in docs/stdlib-api.md. The router is built ONCE, before the
; fork and before any arena mark, exactly as a worker's poll buffers
; are: it is the state a request must not allocate.
;
; STATIC FILES, AND THE ORDER OF THE CHECKS. `routeDispatch` refuses a
; path before ANY route is consulted and before ANY filesystem call:
; it must start with `/`, hold no NUL byte and no `\`, no empty
; segment (`//`) and no `..` segment (`httpPathSafe`). The content
; type is chosen by extension (`httpContentType`) and an unknown
; extension is `application/octet-stream`, never `text/html` - serving
; an unknown file as HTML is the stored-XSS route. A path under a
; static prefix that names nothing, or names a directory, is 404.
;
; KEEP-ALIVE IS NOT HERE, and the reasons are in order of weight.
; MM-ALLOC-22 scopes its own claim to the stateless request: "keep-
; alive, where per-connection state outlives the request, is outside
; the measurement and outside this rule's claim". The reader's buffer
; is allocated inside the request's arena scope, so a second request
; on the same connection would read from reclaimed memory - the hazard
; `Rpc.rdReseat` and `__axiom_arena_reset_keeping` exist to answer,
; and answering it here is a second feature with its own memory arm,
; not a flag. `scripts/check-web.sh` measures one connection per
; request, which is the thing it can see. And closing makes every
; response self-terminating for a client that ignores
; `Content-Length`. RFC 9112 9.6 lets a server close after any
; response, so answering a keep-alive request with `close` conforms.
; The follow-up is stated so it is not rediscovered: `httpRead` over a
; reader re-seated after the reset (`consumed`/`filled` are kept
; relative for it), `__axiom_arena_reset_keeping` for the bytes of the
; next request already read, a per-connection loop, and a keep-alive
; arm in `check-web.sh` whose ablation must grow.
;
; A SPELLING RULE THIS FILE FOLLOWS, learned while writing it: a
; `set` is not a value. Its type is the field's or the binding's own,
; and an `if` whose one arm is a bare `(set x v)` and whose other arm
; is `0` is AX3004 - so every `set` here sits in a `{ ... 0 }` block,
; and every loop flag is an Int compared with `==`, never a Bool.
(import Mem)

(import Str)

(import Vec)

(import Fmt)

(import Sys)

(import Path)

(import Err)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Limits
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The largest request head - request line plus headers plus the blank
; line - `httpRead` will buffer, in bytes: 16 KiB. A head that has not
; ended by then is refused as 431.
(pub :: httpMaxHead Int)

(pub fn (httpMaxHead) 16384)

; The largest `Content-Length` `httpRead` accepts, in bytes: 8 MiB.
; Larger is 413, and so is a value the parser cannot represent.
(pub :: httpMaxBody Int)

(pub fn (httpMaxBody) 8388608)

; The reader's initial buffer, in bytes: 2 KiB, which holds a
; browser's request head with a few cookies in one read (measured in
; the header); the buffer doubles up to `httpMaxHead` when a head does
; not fit.
(pub :: httpReadCap Int)

(pub fn (httpReadCap) 2048)

; `Bool` negation. `==` compares Ints, so `(not b)` is spelled as an
; `if`; one spelling here rather than a dozen inline.
(:: httpNot (-> Bool Bool))

(fn (httpNot x)
  (if x
    false
    true
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The reader
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A buffered reader over a socket: the descriptor, a buffer whose
; `strLen` is its capacity, how much of it holds data, and how far the
; parser has consumed. Bytes between `consumed` and `filled` are the
; request in progress.
(pub struct HttpReader
  (fd : Int)
  (buf : String)
  (filled : Int)
  (consumed : Int))

; A reader over `fd` with the default buffer.
(pub :: httpReaderNew (-> Int HttpReader))

(pub fn (httpReaderNew fd) (httpReaderWith fd httpReadCap))

; A reader over `fd` whose buffer starts at `cap` bytes (at least 1).
; A capacity of 1 makes every `read` answer one byte, which is how
; tests/stdlib/430-http-parse.ax drives the refill loop through every
; boundary a slow peer could put a read on, deterministically and in
; one process.
(pub :: httpReaderWith (-> Int Int HttpReader))

(pub fn (httpReaderWith fd cap)
  (HttpReader
    fd    (strAlloc
      (if (< cap 1)
        1
        cap
      )
    )    0    0
  )
)

; Bytes read but not yet consumed.
(:: httpAvail (-> HttpReader Int))

(fn (httpAvail r) (- r.filled r.consumed))

; Move the live region to the front, then double the buffer if it is
; still full and the ceiling allows. Answers nothing useful.
(:: httpGrow (-> HttpReader Int))

(fn (httpGrow r)
  {
    (if (> r.consumed 0)
      {
        (memCopy (strData r.buf) (+ (strData r.buf) r.consumed) (- r.filled r.consumed))
        (set r.filled (- r.filled r.consumed))
        (set r.consumed 0)
        0
      }
      0
    )
    (if (&& (>= r.filled (strLen r.buf)) (< (strLen r.buf) httpMaxHead))
      (let ((want 
        (if (> (* (strLen r.buf) 2) httpMaxHead)
          httpMaxHead
          (* (strLen r.buf) 2)
        )
      ))
        (let ((bigger (strAlloc want)))
          {
            (memCopy (strData bigger) (strData r.buf) r.filled)
            (set r.buf bigger)
            0
          }
        )
      )
      0
    )
    0
  }
)

; One `read` into the free tail, growing first when the buffer is
; full. Answers the count read: 0 at end of stream, -2 when the buffer
; is full at the ceiling and nothing more can be taken, and the
; syscall's own negative answer for a failed read.
(:: httpFill (-> HttpReader Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (httpFill r)
  {
    (if (>= r.filled (strLen r.buf))
      (httpGrow r)
      0
    )
    (if (>= r.filled (strLen r.buf))
      (- 0 2)
      (let ((n (sysReadFd r.fd (+ (strData r.buf) r.filled) (- (strLen r.buf) r.filled))))
        {
          (if (> n 0)
            {
              (set r.filled (+ r.filled n))
              0
            }
            0
          )
          n
        }
      )
    )
  }
)

; The offset of `\r\n\r\n` within the unconsumed region, relative to
; `consumed` (`Rpc.ax` says why relative: a fill may compact), or -1.
(:: httpHeadEnd (-> HttpReader Int))

(fn (httpHeadEnd r)
  (let (
    (buf r.buf)
    (base r.consumed)
    (n (httpAvail r))
    (mut i 0)
    (mut found (- 0 1))
  )
    {
      (while (&& (< found 0) (<= (+ i 4) n))
        {
          (if (&& (== (strByte buf (+ base i)) 13) (&& (== (strByte buf (+ (+ base i) 1)) 10) (&& (== (strByte buf (+ (+ base i) 2)) 13) (== (strByte buf (+ (+ base i) 3)) 10))))
            {
              (set found i)
              0
            }
            0
          )
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      found
    }
  )
)

; Is there a `\n` not preceded by `\r` in `s` between `from` and `to`?
(:: httpBareLf (-> String Int Int Bool))

(fn (httpBareLf s from to)
  (let (
    (mut i from)
    (mut bare 0)
  )
    {
      (while (&& (< i to) (== bare 0))
        {
          (if (&& (== (strByte s i) 10) (|| (== i from) (!= (strByte s (- i 1)) 13)))
            {
              (set bare 1)
              0
            }
            0
          )
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (== bare 1)
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The request
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; One parsed request. `path` is percent-decoded with the query
; stripped; `query` is the raw bytes after `?` (empty when there were
; none); `hnames` and `hvals` are parallel `Vec`s of Strings, the
; names ASCII-lowercased, so `httpHeader` needs one spelling; `body`
; is exactly `Content-Length` bytes, or empty. Every String here is a
; COPY, never a slice of the reader's buffer, which a later fill may
; move.
(pub struct HttpReq
  (method : String)
  (path : String)
  (query : String)
  (version : String)
  (hnames : Int)
  (hvals : Int)
  (body : String))

; The index of header `name` (lowercased on both sides), or -1.
(:: httpHeaderIndex (-> HttpReq String Int))

(fn (httpHeaderIndex r name)
  (let (
    (want (strLower name))
    (n (vecLen r.hnames))
    (mut i 0)
    (mut found (- 0 1))
  )
    {
      (while (&& (< found 0) (< i n))
        {
          (if (strEq (vecGetStr r.hnames i) want)
            {
              (set found i)
              0
            }
            0
          )
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      found
    }
  )
)

; The value of header `name` (any case; compared lowercased), or
; `dflt` when the request did not carry it. A header sent twice
; answers its first value.
(pub :: httpHeader (-> HttpReq String String String))

(pub fn (httpHeader r name dflt)
  (let ((i (httpHeaderIndex r name)))
    (if (< i 0)
      dflt
      (vecGetStr r.hvals i)
    )
  )
)

; Whether the request carried header `name`, in any case.
(pub :: httpHasHeader (-> HttpReq String Bool))

(pub fn (httpHasHeader r name) (>= (httpHeaderIndex r name) 0))

; Whether the two bytes after a `%` at `i` are hex digits.
(:: httpEscapeAt (-> String Int Bool))

(fn (httpEscapeAt s i) (&& (== (strByte s i) 37) (&& (>= (strHexVal (strByte s (+ i 1))) 0) (>= (strHexVal (strByte s (+ i 2))) 0))))

; `s` percent-decoded: every `%XX` with two hex digits becomes the byte
; `XX`, and when `plusSpace` is set every `+` becomes a space - the
; rule for a query string, and not for a path. A `%` that does not
; start a valid escape is kept as it is rather than refused, so a
; caller that must refuse one compares the answer with the input. Also
; answers `s` itself when there is nothing to decode.
(pub :: httpDecode (-> String Bool String))

(pub fn (httpDecode s plusSpace)
  (let (
    (len (strLen s))
    (mut k 0)
    (mut escapes 0)
    (mut pluses 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< k len)
        (if (httpEscapeAt s k)
          {
            (set escapes (+ escapes 1))
            (set k (+ k 3))
            0
          }
          {
            (if (&& plusSpace (== (strByte s k) 43))
              {
                (set pluses (+ pluses 1))
                0
              }
              0
            )
            (set k (+ k 1))
            0
          }
        ))
      (if (&& (== escapes 0) (== pluses 0))
        s
        (let (
          (out (strAlloc (- len (* escapes 2))))
          (d (strData out))
          (mut i 0)
          (mut off 0)
        )
          {
            (while (< i len)
              (if (httpEscapeAt s i)
                {
                  (__store8 d off (+ (* (strHexVal (strByte s (+ i 1))) 16) (strHexVal (strByte s (+ i 2)))))
                  (set off (+ off 1))
                  (set i (+ i 3))
                  0
                }
                {
                  (__store8
                    d                    off                    (if (&& plusSpace (== (strByte s i) 43))
                      32
                      (strByte s i)
                    )
                  )
                  (set off (+ off 1))
                  (set i (+ i 1))
                  0
                }
              ))
            out
          }
        )
      )
    }
  )
)

; The value of query parameter `name`, decoded (`%XX` and `+`), or
; `dflt` when the query does not carry it. `?q=a%20b&x=1` answers
; `a b` for `q` and `1` for `x`; a pair with no `=` has the empty
; value.
(pub :: httpQueryParam (-> HttpReq String String String))

(pub fn (httpQueryParam r name dflt)
  (let (
    (q r.query)
    (len (strLen r.query))
    (mut at 0)
    (mut answer dflt)
    (mut found 0)
  )
    {
      (while (&& (== found 0) (< at len))
        (let ((amp (strFindByte q 38 at)))
          (let ((end 
            (if (< amp 0)
              len
              amp
            )
          ))
            (let ((eq (strFindByte q 61 at)))
              (let ((keyEnd 
                (if (|| (< eq 0) (> eq end))
                  end
                  eq
                )
              ))
                {
                  (if (strEq (httpDecode (strSlice q at (- keyEnd at)) true) name)
                    {
                      (set found 1)
                      (set answer (if (< keyEnd end)
                        (httpDecode (strSlice q (+ keyEnd 1) (- end (+ keyEnd 1))) true)
                        ""
                      ))
                      0
                    }
                    0
                  )
                  (set at (+ end 1))
                  0
                }
              )
            )
          )
        ))
      answer
    }
  )
)

; A refusal: the HTTP status as the error's code, and what the peer
; will be told as its message.
(:: httpRefuse (-> Int String (Result HttpReq Error)))

(fn (httpRefuse status why) (Err (mkError status why)))

; The header lines of a head that occupies `hdr` bytes past
; `consumed`, starting at absolute offset `at` (just past the request
; line), pushed lowercased into `names` and trimmed into `vals`.
; Answers "" or what was wrong with the first bad line.
(:: httpParseHeaders (-> HttpReader Int Int Int Int String))

(fn (httpParseHeaders r hdr from names vals)
  (let (
    (buf r.buf)
    (stop (+ r.consumed hdr))
    (mut at from)
    (mut problem "")
  )
    {
      (while (&& (== (strLen problem) 0) (< at stop))
        (let ((le (strFindByte buf 13 at)))
          (let ((hl (strSlice buf at (- le at))))
            (let ((colon (strFindByte hl 58 0)))
              {
                (if (< colon 1)
                  {
                    (set problem "header line without a name and a colon")
                    0
                  }
                  (let ((nm (strSlice hl 0 colon)))
                    (if (>= (strFindByte nm 32 0) 0)
                      {
                        (set problem "whitespace in a header name")
                        0
                      }
                      {
                        (vecPush names (strLower (strDup nm)))
                        (vecPush vals (strDup (strTrim (strSlice hl (+ colon 1) (- (strLen hl) (+ colon 1))))))
                        0
                      }
                    )
                  )
                )
                (set at (+ le 2))
                0
              }
            )
          )
        ))
      problem
    }
  )
)

; The head of the request - request line and header lines - parsed
; out of the reader's buffer, where the head occupies `hdr` bytes past
; `consumed` and the terminator follows. Answers the request with an
; empty body, or the refusal.
(:: httpParseHead (-> HttpReader Int (Result HttpReq Error)))

(fn (httpParseHead r hdr)
  (let (
    (buf r.buf)
    (base r.consumed)
  )
    (if (httpBareLf buf base (+ base hdr))
      (httpRefuse 400 "bare LF line ending in the request head")
      (let ((eol (strFindByte buf 13 base)))
        (let ((line (strSlice buf base (- eol base))))
          (let ((sp1 (strFindByte line 32 0)))
            (let ((sp2 
              (if (< sp1 0)
                (- 0 1)
                (strFindByte line 32 (+ sp1 1))
              )
            ))
              (if (|| (< sp1 1) (|| (< sp2 (+ sp1 2)) (|| (>= (strFindByte line 32 (+ sp2 1)) 0) (>= (+ sp2 1) (strLen line)))))
                (httpRefuse 400 "request line is not METHOD TARGET VERSION")
                (let (
                  (method (strDup (strSlice line 0 sp1)))
                  (target (strSlice line (+ sp1 1) (- sp2 (+ sp1 1))))
                  (version (strDup (strSlice line (+ sp2 1) (- (strLen line) (+ sp2 1)))))
                )
                  (if (httpNot (|| (strEq version "HTTP/1.1") (strEq version "HTTP/1.0")))
                    (httpRefuse 505 "only HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1 are served")
                    (let ((qm (strFindByte target 63 0)))
                      (let (
                        (path (httpDecode
                          (strDup
                            (if (< qm 0)
                              target
                              (strSlice target 0 qm)
                            )
                          )                          false
                        ))
                        (query (strDup
                          (if (< qm 0)
                            ""
                            (strSlice target (+ qm 1) (- (strLen target) (+ qm 1)))
                          )
                        ))
                        (names vecNew)
                        (vals vecNew)
                      )
                        (let ((problem (httpParseHeaders
                          r                          hdr                          (+ eol 2)                          names                          vals
                        )))
                          (if (> (strLen problem) 0)
                            (httpRefuse 400 problem)
                            (Ok (HttpReq
                              method                              path                              query                              version                              names                              vals                              ""
                            ))
                          )
                        )
                      )
                    )
                  )
                )
              )
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; Read `n` bytes of body for `req` out of the reader and then straight
; off the socket into a buffer of exactly `n` bytes, and answer the
; request with its body, or the refusal when the peer closed first.
(:: httpReadBody (-> HttpReader HttpReq Int (Result HttpReq Error)))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (httpReadBody r req n)
  (let (
    (body (strAlloc n))
    (have (if (> (httpAvail r) n)
      n
      (httpAvail r)
    ))
    (mut got 0)
    (mut dead 0)
  )
    {
      (memCopy (strData body) (+ (strData r.buf) r.consumed) have)
      (set r.consumed (+ r.consumed have))
      (set got have)
      (while (&& (< got n) (== dead 0))
        (let ((k (sysReadFd r.fd (+ (strData body) got) (- n got))))
          (if (< k 1)
            {
              (set dead 1)
              0
            }
            {
              (set got (+ got k))
              0
            }
          )
        ))
      (if (== dead 1)
        (httpRefuse 400 "connection closed before Content-Length bytes of body arrived")
        {
          (set req.body body)
          (Ok req)
        }
      )
    }
  )
)

; The body a parsed head calls for: none without `Content-Length`,
; else exactly that many bytes, with the ceilings applied first.
(:: httpReadRest (-> HttpReader HttpReq (Result HttpReq Error)))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (httpReadRest r req)
  (if (httpHasHeader req "transfer-encoding")
    (httpRefuse 501 "Transfer-Encoding is not served; send Content-Length")
    (if (httpHasHeader req "content-length")
      (match (strParseInt (httpHeader req "content-length" ""))
        ((None) (httpRefuse 400 "Content-Length is not a decimal integer"))
        ((Some n)
          (if (< n 0)
            (httpRefuse 400 "Content-Length is negative")
            (if (> n httpMaxBody)
              (httpRefuse 413 "Content-Length above httpMaxBody")
              (httpReadBody r req n)
            )
          )
        )
      )
      (Ok req)
    )
  )
)

; Read one whole request from the reader: refill until the head has
; ended, parse it, then read exactly `Content-Length` bytes of body.
; The refusals answer an `Error` whose CODE is the HTTP status to
; write back: 400 for a malformed head or a peer that closed early,
; 413 for a body above `httpMaxBody` or a length the parser cannot
; hold, 431 for a head above `httpMaxHead`, 501 for
; `Transfer-Encoding` (chunked bodies are not read), 505 for a version
; that is not HTTP/1.x.
(pub :: httpRead (-> HttpReader (Result HttpReq Error)))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (httpRead r)
  (let (
    (mut hdr (- 0 1))
    (mut stop 0)
  )
    {
      (while (&& (< hdr 0) (== stop 0))
        {
          (set hdr (httpHeadEnd r))
          (if (< hdr 0)
            (let ((n (httpFill r)))
              (if (< n 1)
                {
                  (set stop (if (== n (- 0 2))
                    431
                    400
                  ))
                  0
                }
                0
              )
            )
            0
          )
        })
      (if (== stop 431)
        (httpRefuse 431 "request head larger than httpMaxHead")
        (if (== stop 400)
          (httpRefuse 400 "connection closed before the request head was complete")
          (match (httpParseHead r hdr)
            ((Err e) (Err e))
            ((Ok req)
              {
                (set r.consumed (+ r.consumed (+ hdr 4)))
                (httpReadRest r req)
              }
            )
          )
        )
      )
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The response
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The reason phrase for a status, or `Unknown` for one this module
; does not name.
(pub :: httpStatusText (-> Int String))

(pub fn (httpStatusText status)
  (if (== status 200)
    "OK"
    (if (== status 201)
      "Created"
      (if (== status 204)
        "No Content"
        (if (== status 301)
          "Moved Permanently"
          (if (== status 302)
            "Found"
            (if (== status 304)
              "Not Modified"
              (if (== status 400)
                "Bad Request"
                (if (== status 403)
                  "Forbidden"
                  (if (== status 404)
                    "Not Found"
                    (if (== status 405)
                      "Method Not Allowed"
                      (if (== status 413)
                        "Content Too Large"
                        (if (== status 431)
                          "Request Header Fields Too Large"
                          (if (== status 500)
                            "Internal Server Error"
                            (if (== status 501)
                              "Not Implemented"
                              (if (== status 505)
                                "HTTP Version Not Supported"
                                "Unknown"
                              )
                            )
                          )
                        )
                      )
                    )
                  )
                )
              )
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; The `Content-Type` for a file name, by its extension: `.html`,
; `.css`, `.js`, `.svg`, `.png`, `.ico`, `.txt` and `.json` are named,
; and everything else is `application/octet-stream` - deliberately not
; `text/html`, which is the stored-XSS route for an unknown file.
(pub :: httpContentType (-> String String))

(pub fn (httpContentType name)
  (let ((ext (pathExt name)))
    (if (strEq ext ".html")
      "text/html; charset=utf-8"
      (if (strEq ext ".css")
        "text/css; charset=utf-8"
        (if (strEq ext ".js")
          "text/javascript; charset=utf-8"
          (if (strEq ext ".svg")
            "image/svg+xml"
            (if (strEq ext ".png")
              "image/png"
              (if (strEq ext ".ico")
                "image/vnd.microsoft.icon"
                (if (strEq ext ".txt")
                  "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
                  (if (strEq ext ".json")
                    "application/json"
                    "application/octet-stream"
                  )
                )
              )
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; The pieces of a `Vec` of Strings as one String: one allocation and
; one copy per piece, `Json.ax`'s `jsonJoin`.
(:: httpJoin (-> Int String))

(fn (httpJoin pieces)
  (let (
    (mut k 0)
    (mut total 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< k (vecLen pieces))
        {
          (set total (+ total (strLen (vecGetStr pieces k))))
          (set k (+ k 1))
        })
      (let (
        (out (strAlloc total))
        (mut j 0)
        (mut off 0)
      )
        {
          (while (< j (vecLen pieces))
            (let ((p (vecGetStr pieces j)))
              {
                (memCopy (+ (strData out) off) (strData p) (strLen p))
                (set off (+ off (strLen p)))
                (set j (+ j 1))
              }
            ))
          out
        }
      )
    }
  )
)

; The response head: status line, `Content-Type`, `Content-Length`,
; `Connection: close`, blank line. CRLF throughout.
(:: httpHead (-> Int String Int String))

(fn (httpHead status ctype len)
  (let ((pieces vecNew))
    {
      (vecPush pieces "HTTP/1.1 ")
      (vecPush pieces (fmtInt status))
      (vecPush pieces " ")
      (vecPush pieces (httpStatusText status))
      (vecPush pieces "\r\nContent-Type: ")
      (vecPush pieces ctype)
      (vecPush pieces "\r\nContent-Length: ")
      (vecPush pieces (fmtInt len))
      (vecPush pieces "\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n")
      (httpJoin pieces)
    }
  )
)

; Write a whole response to `fd`: the head for `status` and `ctype`
; with `Content-Length: len`, then the `len` bytes at `addr`. Every
; write goes through `sysWriteAllFd`. Takes an address and a length
; rather than a String so that a body holding a NUL byte - a PNG - is
; written whole; `strCStr` would stop at the NUL. Answers what the
; body's `sysWriteAllFd` answered, the head's when that one failed.
(pub :: httpRespondRaw (-> Int Int String Int Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (httpRespondRaw fd status ctype addr len)
  (let ((head (httpHead status ctype len)))
    (let ((w (sysWriteAllFd fd (strData head) (strLen head) 0)))
      (if (< w (strLen head))
        w
        (sysWriteAllFd fd addr len 0)
      )
    )
  )
)

; Write a whole response whose body is the String `body`:
; `httpRespondRaw` over its bytes and its byte count.
(pub :: httpRespond (-> Int Int String String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (httpRespond fd status ctype body) (httpRespondRaw
  fd  status  ctype  (strData body)  (strLen body)
))

; A plain-text refusal or error page: `status` with its reason phrase
; and `why` as the body, so a curl user reads the reason on the
; terminal. Answers `status`.
(pub :: httpFail (-> Int Int String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (httpFail fd status why)
  (let ((pieces vecNew))
    {
      (vecPush pieces (fmtInt status))
      (vecPush pieces " ")
      (vecPush pieces (httpStatusText status))
      (vecPush pieces ": ")
      (vecPush pieces why)
      (vecPush pieces "\n")
      (httpRespond fd status "text/plain; charset=utf-8" (httpJoin pieces))
      status
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The router
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A handler: a function of the socket and the request, answering an
; Int the dispatcher passes back. Held in a cell because the router
; keeps handlers in a `Vec` of words. Written as
; `(HttpFn (lambda (fd r) (page fd r)))` around a signed `fn`.
(pub data HttpHandler
  (HttpFn (-> Int HttpReq Int)))

; The routing table: exact routes as three parallel `Vec`s (method,
; path, handler cell), static prefixes as two (URL prefix, directory),
; and the handler for a path nothing matched.
(pub struct HttpRouter
  (methods : Int)
  (paths : Int)
  (handlers : Int)
  (prefixes : Int)
  (dirs : Int)
  (missing : HttpHandler))

; The default not-found handler: a plain-text 404.
(:: httpDefaultMissing (-> Int HttpReq Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (httpDefaultMissing fd r) (httpFail fd 404 "no route for this path"))

; An empty router whose not-found handler writes a plain-text 404.
; `IO`, because the checker charges a function with the effects of
; the lambda it builds, and the default handler writes.
(pub :: routerNew HttpRouter)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (routerNew)
  (HttpRouter
    vecNew    vecNew    vecNew    vecNew    vecNew    (HttpFn
      (lambda (fd r) (httpDefaultMissing fd r))
    )
  )
)

; Route `method` (`GET`, `POST`, ...) at exactly `path` to `h`. Routes
; are tried in the order they were added. Answers the route's index.
(pub :: routeAdd (-> HttpRouter String String HttpHandler Int))

(pub fn (routeAdd rt method path h)
  {
    (vecPush rt.methods method)
    (vecPush rt.paths path)
    (vecPush rt.handlers h)
    (- (vecLen rt.paths) 1)
  }
)

; Serve GET requests under URL `prefix` (write it with its trailing
; slash: `/static/`) from the files under directory `dir`. Answers the
; mapping's index.
(pub :: routeStatic (-> HttpRouter String String Int))

(pub fn (routeStatic rt prefix dir)
  {
    (vecPush rt.prefixes prefix)
    (vecPush rt.dirs dir)
    (- (vecLen rt.prefixes) 1)
  }
)

; Replace the not-found handler.
(pub :: routeNotFound (-> HttpRouter HttpHandler Int))

(pub fn (routeNotFound rt h)
  {
    (set rt.missing h)
    0
  }
)

; The handler cell at route `i`. The cast is at a RETURN inside a
; signature that carries the type, the position `vecGetStr` uses.
(:: routeHandlerAt (-> HttpRouter Int HttpHandler))

(fn (routeHandlerAt rt i)
  (cast HttpHandler (vecGet rt.handlers i))
)

; Call a handler cell.
(:: httpCall (-> HttpHandler Int HttpReq Int))

(fn (httpCall h fd r)
  (match h
    ((HttpFn f) (f fd r))
  )
)

; Whether a request path may reach the filesystem or a route at all:
; it starts with `/`, holds no NUL and no `\`, and has no empty
; segment (`//`) and no `..` segment. Decided on the DECODED path, so
; `%2e%2e` is `..` here.
(pub :: httpPathSafe (-> String Bool))

(pub fn (httpPathSafe p)
  ; `seg` is where the current segment started; a segment ends at
  ; every `/` and at the end of the path
  (let (
    (len (strLen p))
    (mut i 1)
    (mut ok (if (&& (> (strLen p) 0) (== (strByte p 0) 47))
      1
      0
    ))
    (mut seg 1)
  )
    {
      (while (&& (== ok 1) (<= i len))
        (let ((c 
          (if (== i len)
            47
            (strByte p i)
          )
        ))
          {
            (if (|| (== c 0) (== c 92))
              {
                (set ok 0)
                0
              }
              (if (== c 47)
                {
                  (if (&& (== seg i) (< i len))
                    {
                      (set ok 0)
                      0
                    }
                    0
                  )
                  (if (&& (== (- i seg) 2) (&& (== (strByte p seg) 46) (== (strByte p (+ seg 1)) 46)))
                    {
                      (set ok 0)
                      0
                    }
                    0
                  )
                  (set seg (+ i 1))
                  0
                }
                0
              )
            )
            (set i (+ i 1))
            0
          }
        ))
      (== ok 1)
    }
  )
)

; Serve the file that `req`'s path names under `prefix` out of `dir`
; to `fd`: 400 when the path is unsafe, 404 when the rest of the path
; is empty or names nothing or a directory, otherwise 200 with the
; content type of its extension and its bytes written whole. The path
; is checked before the filesystem is touched.
(pub :: httpServeFile (-> Int HttpReq String String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (httpServeFile fd req prefix dir)
  (if (httpPathSafe req.path)
    (let ((rest (strSlice req.path (strLen prefix) (- (strLen req.path) (strLen prefix)))))
      (if (== (strLen rest) 0)
        (httpFail fd 404 "no file named")
        (let ((full (pathJoin dir rest)))
          (if (sysFileExists (strCStr full))
            (if (sysIsDir (strCStr full))
              (httpFail fd 404 "that is a directory")
              (let ((bytes (sysReadFile (strCStr full))))
                (httpRespondRaw
                  fd                  200                  (httpContentType full)                  (strData bytes)                  (strLen bytes)
                )
              )
            )
            (httpFail fd 404 "no such file")
          )
        )
      )
    )
    (httpFail fd 400 "unsafe path")
  )
)

; The index of the exact route for `method` and `path`, or -1.
(:: routeFind (-> HttpRouter String String Int))

(fn (routeFind rt method path)
  (let (
    (n (vecLen rt.paths))
    (mut i 0)
    (mut found (- 0 1))
  )
    {
      (while (&& (< found 0) (< i n))
        {
          (if (&& (strEq (vecGetStr rt.paths i) path) (strEq (vecGetStr rt.methods i) method))
            {
              (set found i)
              0
            }
            0
          )
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      found
    }
  )
)

; Whether any route, whatever its method, is at exactly `path` - the
; 405 question.
(:: routeHasPath (-> HttpRouter String Bool))

(fn (routeHasPath rt path)
  (let (
    (n (vecLen rt.paths))
    (mut i 0)
    (mut found 0)
  )
    {
      (while (&& (== found 0) (< i n))
        {
          (if (strEq (vecGetStr rt.paths i) path)
            {
              (set found 1)
              0
            }
            0
          )
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (== found 1)
    }
  )
)

; The index of the static prefix `path` starts with, or -1.
(:: routeFindStatic (-> HttpRouter String Int))

(fn (routeFindStatic rt path)
  (let (
    (n (vecLen rt.prefixes))
    (mut i 0)
    (mut found (- 0 1))
  )
    {
      (while (&& (< found 0) (< i n))
        {
          (if (strStartsWith path (vecGetStr rt.prefixes i))
            {
              (set found i)
              0
            }
            0
          )
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      found
    }
  )
)

; Answer `req` on `fd`: an unsafe path is 400 before anything else is
; consulted; then the exact routes, in order; then the static
; prefixes, for GET (405 otherwise); then 405 when the path has a
; route for another method; then the not-found handler. Answers what
; the handler answered.
(pub :: routeDispatch (-> HttpRouter Int HttpReq Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (routeDispatch rt fd req)
  (if (httpPathSafe req.path)
    (let ((i (routeFind rt req.method req.path)))
      (if (>= i 0)
        (httpCall (routeHandlerAt rt i) fd req)
        (let ((s (routeFindStatic rt req.path)))
          (if (>= s 0)
            (if (strEq req.method "GET")
              (httpServeFile fd req (vecGetStr rt.prefixes s) (vecGetStr rt.dirs s))
              (httpFail fd 405 "static files are served for GET")
            )
            (if (routeHasPath rt req.path)
              (httpFail fd 405 "no route for this method at this path")
              (httpCall rt.missing fd req)
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
    (httpFail fd 400 "unsafe path")
  )
)

; One connection, start to finish: read the request off `fd`, and
; either dispatch it or write the parser's refusal back with the
; status the error carries. Answers the handler's answer, or the
; status written for a refusal. The caller owns the socket - it set it
; blocking, and it closes it - and the arena scope around this call is
; the caller's too.
(pub :: httpServeOne (-> HttpRouter Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (httpServeOne rt fd)
  (match (httpRead (httpReaderNew fd))
    ((Ok req) (routeDispatch rt fd req))
    ((Err e) (httpFail fd (errCode e) (errMessage e)))
  )
)
